  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   The central idea is to evolve the parent solution by gradually adjusting its step locations to minimize maximum overlap. We use a multi-resolution strategy to refine the solution starting from the base 380-point parent representation. Key rationales:\n   - The parent solution's step structure provides a good baseline\n   - Gradual adjustments better escaping local optima than coarse binary optimizations\n   - Successive resolutions allow refined optimization after structure preservation\n   - Gradient-based moves give better convergence than random binary moves\n\n   The approach overcomes limitations seen in previous strategies by:\n   - Using continuous space optimization to maintain integral constraints properly\n   - Applying structured gradient descent to reduce maximum overlap\n   - Using a fallback repair mechanism to handle constraint violations during optimization\n   - Maintaining a consistent representation across multi-resolution transfers\n   Key bottleneck addressed: poor integral preservation in previous implementations\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   Representation:\n   - h(x) represented by N samples on [0,2] (N=380 initially)\n   - dx = 2.0/N\n   - Sum(h[i])*dx = 1\n   - h[i] \u2208 [0,1]\n\n   Objective:\n   - C5 = max_k \u222b_{-k}^{2-k} h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n   - Discretized as max_lag (np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\") * dx\n\n   Optimization:\n   - Minimize C5\n   - Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and sum(h[i])*dx = 1\n   - Gradient descent formulation to enforce integral preservation\n\n   Transfer:\n   - Linear interpolation for multi-resolution\n   - Maintain discrete constraint preservation through projection\n   - Integral adjusted by scaling: sum(h[i])*dx = 1\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   Step 1: Initialize parent solution (380 points)\n      - Use provided initial_h_values\n      - Set N=380, dx=2/380\n      - Compute initial C5\n      - Project to binary constraints for initial state\n\n   Step 2: Define helper functions\n      - transfer_solution(parent, resolution, max_time):\n            Interpolate parent to new resolution (N=resolution)\n            Adjust integral to preserve sum(h[i])*dx=1\n            Clip values to [0,1]\n            Return transferred solution\n\n      - repair_feasibility(h, N, max_time):\n            Violation check for boundary values\n            Repair violations using reflection/perturbation\n            Maintain integral preservation\n\n      - gradient_descent_opt(h, N, max_time, steps=50):\n            Use Adam optimizer with projection to [0,1] and integral one\n            Update rule: h <- clip( h + learning_rate * gradient )\n            Gradient from: \u2207C5 = correlate(mask * h, gradient_kernel, mode=\"full\")\n            Include learning_rate scheduling\n\n   Step 3: Multi-resolution optimization\n      Main resolution sequence: 380, 760, 1520 (doubling steps)\n      Starting from parent solution:\n      - Optimize at 380 points for 500 seconds using gradient descent\n      - Transfer to 760 points, optimize for 350 seconds\n      - Transfer to 1520 points, optimize for 250 seconds\n      Time budget: 1100 seconds\n\n      Termination:\n        When target improvement threshold (0.38087 above initial) achieved\n        Or time budget exhausted\n\n   Step 4: Candidate acceptance\n      Keep best solution if C5 value improves\n      Check solution validity at each step\n      Preserve distribution of 0/1/intermediate values across resolutions\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n   - Optimization schedule:\n        Initial phase: 500 seconds optimization at 380 points\n        Middle phases: 350 seconds at 760 points\n        Late phase: 250 seconds at 1520 points\n        Minimum operation time: 60 seconds per grid\n\n   - Parameter settings:\n        Learning rate: 0.02 (initial)\n        Beta: 0.9 (momentum)\n        History size: 50 (gradient history)\n        Lag kernel: Gaussian with 3\u03c3\n\n   - Fallback strategy:\n        If optimization stalls (no improvement 200 seconds)\n        Repair solution by flipping 10% of positions\n        Reset gradient history\n\n   - Solution rollback:\n        Every 100 seconds check improvement\n        If no improvement, restart from best kept earlier state\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n   - Time breakdown:\n        Initialization: 30 seconds\n        Optimization phases: 500 + 350 + 250 = 1100 seconds\n        Validation: 20 seconds reserve\n\n   - Memory constraints:\n        No focus on memory since N up to 1520 (\u223c6K points)\n\n   - Deadline handling:\n        Use wall clock time tracking\n        Break early if target achieved\n        Fallback to previous best on schedule deadline\n\n   - Robustness checks:\n        Violation repair on ill-conditioned moves\n        History clipping for numerical stability\n        Parameter adaptation on gradient explosion\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Feasibility checker:\n        All values: 0 \u2264 x \u2264 1\n        Integral: |sum(h[i])*dx - 1| < 1e-5\n        Overlap: C5 strictly decreases\n\n   - Metric consistency:\n        C5 computed as: max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\"))*(2/N)\n\n   - Output format:\n        h[:]: properly formatted 380-point array\n        C5: computed with dx adjustment\n        N: finest grid used (1520)\n        Projected deadline: 1100s check points\n\n   - Detailed output checks:\n        Solution encoded as continuous endpoints but satisfying binary constraints\n        Stats: min/mean/max of h, correlation response\n\n   End-to-end reliability:\n     0. Setup clock and allocations\n     1. Initialize and evaluate parent\n     2. Check feasibility and base state\n     3. Proceed resolutions with attention to improvements\n     4. Verify and repair on each optimization exit\n     5. Select best solution (lowest C5)\n\n   Note: Functions should be defined accordingly",
